---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'

/*
 * Two reads of the same entity, an hour apart. The point of the transcript is
 * the pair of lines a reader would otherwise have to derive: `notes_needed`
 * counting down, and the entity's overall readiness sitting at the least
 * advanced of its aspects rather than at the best one. Every field name and
 * every value is one the specification publishes, and the arithmetic holds
 * against the floor of 50 the same document states.
 */
const progress = [
  { cmd: 'E="project=support&entity_kind=agent&entity_name=support_agent"' },
  { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/v1/api/aspects/progress?$E"' },
  { out: '"readiness": "collecting",' },
  { out: '"aspects": [' },
  { out: '  { "aspect": "goal",      "readiness": "collecting", "note_count":  31, "notes_needed": 19 },' },
  { out: '  { "aspect": "behaviour", "readiness": "collecting", "note_count":   8, "notes_needed": 42 },' },
  { out: '  { "aspect": "outcome",   "readiness": "ready",      "note_count": 812, "notes_needed":  0 }]' },
  { cmd: '# an hour later, the same request' },
  { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/v1/api/aspects/progress?$E"' },
  { out: '"readiness": "processing",', tone: 'ok' },
  { out: '"aspects": [' },
  { out: '  { "aspect": "goal",      "readiness": "processing", "note_count":   63, "notes_needed": 0 },' },
  { out: '  { "aspect": "behaviour", "readiness": "processing", "note_count":   57, "notes_needed": 0 },' },
  { out: '  { "aspect": "outcome",   "readiness": "ready",      "note_count": 1204, "notes_needed": 0 }]' },
]
---

<Doc
  title="Themes and snapshots"
  description="How aspect notes are grouped by shared wording, the floor before anything is reported, what collecting, processing and ready each mean, and what a snapshot is."
  lede="A theme is a group of aspect notes that share their wording. There is no model in it and no embedding in it, so every group is arithmetic you can check."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>How a note becomes a theme</h2>
  <p>
    Clustering runs once per aspect, per entity, on the daemon's own timer. An
    entity is the pair of a kind and a name, which is the identity spans already
    carry, so nothing new has to be assigned to have one. A pass reads that
    entity's notes along one aspect, newest first, groups them, and writes the
    result as a snapshot.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it compares is a sketch of the wording, not the wording itself. When a
    note is stored the daemon normalises the text, lowercases it, takes its
    one-word and two-word runs, hashes them with a fixed seed and keeps the 64
    smallest values. That is 512 bytes a note, in an ordinary binary column on
    all three storage backends. There is no vector type, no distance function
    in SQL and no extension to install, which is what keeps mcpd one binary
    with SQLite linked into it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two notes are compared by counting how many of those 64 values they share.
    The answer is a whole number out of 64 and there is not one floating-point
    operation in it, so two replicas of one deployment reading the same notes
    cannot disagree about where a group ends. Notes are then gathered around
    leaders, with the order of every tie stated rather than left to the
    database, and each group's label is the note most like the rest of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    A sketch stays inside the daemon. It is deliberately not a field on a note,
    so the hashing can change without breaking a caller.
  </p>

  <h2>Grouping by wording</h2>
  <p>
    A theme groups aspect notes that share their wording. That is why mcpd needs
    no model and no embedding to build one. The daemon compares wording, not
    meaning, so two notes that mean the same thing in different words land in
    separate themes, and a theme's label is the note most like the others
    rather than a summary written for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three things follow from that, and each one changes how a screen should be
    read.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The same problem can appear twice.</strong> "The order never
      arrived" and "the parcel was not delivered" are one complaint and two
      themes. A count is a count of one wording, never of one meaning.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The label is an example, not a description.</strong> It is the
      text of a real note about a real trace, and
      <code>label_trace_id</code> names that run, so you can open it rather than
      take the label's word for the group. It also changes between snapshots as
      the group does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing here is an opinion about your agent.</strong> The daemon
      grouped sentences it was given. Whether those sentences were right about
      the runs is settled on the run, which is what
      <a href="/docs/aspects" class="link">Aspect notes</a> is about.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The answer is checkable. Every step from a
    stored sentence to a group on screen is arithmetic you could redo by hand,
    which is not true of a clustering that runs through a model whose weights
    you do not have and whose version you cannot pin.
  </p>

  <h2>The floor, and the ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    Below 50 notes for one aspect on one entity, no pass runs and no snapshot is
    written. That is a refusal to answer, not an empty answer, and it is
    deliberate: a dozen sentences can always be sorted into groups, and the
    result looks exactly like a finding. The floor is the line under which mcpd
    would rather say nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bounds come back with the catalogue, at
    <code>GET /v1/api/aspects</code>, because each one changes what a reader is
    looking at. They are copied from the divergence watch, which is the one
    windowed statistic mcpd already ships.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Bound</th><th>Default</th><th>What it changes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>min_notes_to_cluster</code></td>
        <td>50</td>
        <td>Below it the aspect reads <code>collecting</code> and there is no snapshot to read.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>max_notes_per_pass</code></td>
        <td>20,000</td>
        <td>A window holding more is clustered from the newest of them, and the snapshot is marked <code>capped</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>page_size</code></td>
        <td>500</td>
        <td>How much a pass holds at a time. It bounds memory, not coverage.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>max_aspects</code></td>
        <td>32</td>
        <td>How many aspects one project may have, the four built-ins included. A fixed value.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <code>capped</code> is the field to read before you quote a percentage. Every
    share in a snapshot is a share of <code>note_count</code>, the notes the pass
    actually read, and never of the notes in the window. On a capped snapshot
    those are different numbers, and reading a coverage figure as a share of the
    window overstates it.
  </p>

  <h2>The three readiness states</h2>
  <p>
    An empty themes screen has more than one cause, and the causes need different
    actions. <code>GET /v1/api/aspects/progress</code> separates them, counted
    from stored rows rather than estimated, so it cannot go stale.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>State</th><th>What is true</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>collecting</code></td>
        <td>Fewer notes than the floor. No pass has run and none will yet.</td>
        <td>Wait, or write more notes. <code>notes_needed</code> says how many.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>processing</code></td>
        <td>Enough notes, and no snapshot covers this aspect yet.</td>
        <td>Nothing. The next pass writes one.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ready</code></td>
        <td>A snapshot covers this aspect. Every theme route answers.</td>
        <td>Read it. An empty screen now means the wrong aspect or the wrong entity.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Readiness is per entity and per aspect, never global. One agent can have goal
    themes to read while its behaviour notes are still collecting, and an entity's
    own state is the least advanced of its enabled aspects rather than the best
    one, so a row that says <code>processing</code> is telling you that at least
    one aspect is still short of a snapshot.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast class="not-prose my-8" title="watching an entity fill" lines={progress} />

  <p>
    One case sits outside the three states, and it is the one most often
    mistaken for <code>collecting</code>: an entity nobody has written about yet
    has no row at all. A row appears in
    <code>GET /v1/api/aspects/entities</code> the moment the first note about
    that entity is recorded, well before any theme exists. Nothing has been
    written here, and something is being written and there is not enough of it,
    are different facts about a deployment, and a screen with a count moving on
    it reads differently from a screen with nothing on it. That is why the row
    arrives early, and why it carries <code>ready_aspects</code> beside
    <code>enabled_aspects</code>: the two read as "2 of 4" and answer whether the
    row is worth opening.
  </p>
  <p>
    A refusal is different again, and it never changes. A storage backend that
    does not implement this family answers every route in it with 501, naming
    <code>theme_analysis</code> in the body, which is the same word
    <code>GET /v1/api/capabilities</code> publishes. Read the capability set once
    at connect time and decide whether to offer the feature at all, rather than
    retrying a refusal that will never change.
  </p>

  <h2>A snapshot is one pass over a moving window</h2>
  <p>
    A snapshot is the result of one clustering pass over one entity, covering a
    window of notes. It is what turns a clustering into a series: every theme
    belongs to exactly one snapshot, and every theme query names one.
  </p>
  <p>
    It carries the window it read (<code>window_from</code> and
    <code>window_to</code>), the moment it became the current state
    (<code>cutoff_at</code>), where it falls in the entity's series
    (<code>ordinal</code>), how many notes it read and how many traces those
    notes were about, and <code>capped</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Windows overlap, so counts from two snapshots must never be added.</strong>
    A trace whose note falls inside three windows is counted once in each of
    them, and summing the three counts it three times. Compare snapshots, or read
    one. Do not total them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reads never recompute. A pass writes a snapshot on its own timer and every
    read is a lookup of what it wrote, so refreshing a page shows the same
    numbers rather than a fresh clustering that has quietly moved under you. If
    you want a newer answer you wait for the next pass, and the timeline tells
    you when the last one was.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a series of them</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/themes/snapshots</code> returns an entity's passes, oldest
    first, in one of two presentations. An <code>inventory</code> returns
    consecutive snapshots, which is what a caller walking the whole series wants.
    <code>landmarks</code> returns a bounded selection spread across the range,
    with the oldest and the newest always in it and the rest sampled by time, so
    a quiet week is not crowded out by an hour of frequent passes. Each landmark
    row says why it was chosen, as the start of the range, the end of it, or a
    time sample between the two, and an inventory chooses nothing so its rows
    carry no reason at all. <code>total</code> says how many snapshots the
    selection was drawn from, so a timeline can state what it stands in for.
  </p>
  <p>
    One theme's own series is <code>GET /v1/api/themes/{'{theme_id}'}/history</code>,
    the only theme route that names no snapshot, because the series is the
    answer. A theme keeps its id while its wording holds, so the same id in
    two snapshots is the same theme, and the history records what happened
    between each pair: <code>birth</code>, <code>continue</code>,
    <code>split</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>resurrection</code> and
    <code>death</code>. A theme that comes and goes reads as one theme with gaps
    rather than as several short-lived ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retention bounds what that can tell you. The oldest point returned is the
    oldest snapshot still stored, which is a lower bound on the theme's age and
    not its birth. A series that begins at the edge of the window is telling you
    when mcpd started looking, not when the pattern started.
  </p>

  <h2>Across aspects: noise, overflow and association</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/themes/flow</code> reads one snapshot across two to four
    aspects at once, as a stage per aspect and links between neighbouring stages.
    A link's count is the number of traces assigned to both of its ends.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>That is association and nothing else.</strong> A ribbon between a
    goal theme and an outcome theme means the two were written about the same
    runs. It does not mean one caused the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The order you ask for the aspects in is part of the question, not a display
    preference. Links only ever join neighbouring stages, so goal beside outcome
    and goal beside behaviour are two different questions with two different
    answers. Fewer than two aspects is refused by name rather than answered with
    an empty flow, because a single stage has no links in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each stage holds its themes and at most two other nodes, and the three
    together account for every trace in the stage, which is what lets the shares
    add up.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Noise</strong> is the notes that joined no theme. It is not a
      failure bucket. It holds rare requests, wording nothing else matched, and
      patterns that have not recurred yet, so a large noise share means varied
      traffic or too few notes rather than something going wrong. Its sentences
      are read from the examples route with <code>noise</code> as the theme id;
      there is no second pair of routes for it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Overflow</strong> is everything past
      <code>theme_limit_per_stage</code>, folded into one node so the stage
      totals still add up. It carries no theme id and cannot be opened or
      filtered on, because it is a fold for the screen and not a group of
      anything.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    From any theme, <code>GET /v1/api/themes/{'{theme_id}'}/examples</code>
    returns the sentences that put traces there, most alike first, each with the
    run it was written about. That is the route back out of the aggregate and
    into the recording, and it is where a grouping you do not believe gets
    settled.
  </p>

  <h2>Two replicas cannot disagree</h2>
  <p>
    Determinism here has two halves, and both are needed. The clustering is a
    pure function of the notes it reads, with no floating-point arithmetic in the
    comparison and every tie broken by a stated rule, so two daemons in front of
    one database write the same themes. And reads never recompute, so a screen
    refreshed twice shows one answer rather than two.
  </p>
  <p>
    All three storage backends give the same themes, because the clustering runs
    in the daemon over stored notes and not in SQL. There is nothing here for a
    backend to do differently, and one case in the shared conformance suite
    proves it rather than asserting it.
  </p>

  <h2>A daemon with nothing configured</h2>
  <p>
    The pass runs only when <code>aspects.enabled</code> is set in the config
    file. Without it no pass runs at all: nothing is clustered, no outcome note
    is written, nothing is spent and no timer fires, in the same way the monitor
    loop is never spawned when nothing is armed. Notes your process posts are
    still stored and can be read back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Setting it is the only configuration the analysis needs.
    <code>outcome</code> ships derived and is written by the daemon itself with
    no model anywhere, so a deployment that sets <code>aspects.enabled</code>
    and configures nothing else still fills one aspect from the runs it already
    has. That is enough for a real themes screen and enough to see the whole
    pipeline work before a model is wired to anything.
  </p>

